 ## Background
-The concept of feed-forward 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged to address the computational bottleneck of the original 3DGS method, which requires iterative per-scene optimization. The term "Generalizable Reconstruction Method" (GRM) appears in literature describing systems that utilize existing multi-view generation models to train a pixel-aligned Gaussian representation for faithful 3D generation using a single feed-forward pass [S1, unofficial]. This approach shifts the computational load from inference time (per-scene optimization) to training time (learning priors from large datasets), enabling real-time novel view synthesis and reconstruction for unseen scenes.
+The method originates from the need to overcome the computational latency of traditional 3D Gaussian Splatting, which requires per-scene optimization. GRM [S1, unofficial] utilizes existing multi-view generation models to train a pixel-aligned Gaussian representation for faithful 3D generation using a single feed-forward pass.
 
